uestion/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b8974fc-30d6-5417-a48d-1b3409dc7e93" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{18s + 3t = 3; 3s - t = -1$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $18$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}-6}$, then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $-18$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $18$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{18s + 3t = 3; (3s - t = -1)\\cdot (-6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18s + 3t &= 3 \\\\\n-18s + 6t &= 6 \\\\\n(3t + 6t) &= (3 + 6) \\\\\n9t &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $s$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93dcc73c-ce2d-5fa3-b32b-9fc600fb269e" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d30a9310-132f-58dd-adc9-7ca05291ae85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509211} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd64ef7-1c64-574c-8ea8-0d518b6c5c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "first" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509236}] :answer-field/