wer.type/text} :answer/value "first" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509158} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8160bd8-72f0-543f-a4bb-48e47b164659" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a39a169-0a8a-5634-a2bd-69230a2e80b3" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-x + 6y = -10; 3x - 6y = -10$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$, then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-3$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-x + 6y = -10)\\cdot (3); 3x - 6y = -10\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 18y &= -30 \\\\\n3x - 6y &= -10 \\\\\n(18y - 6y) &= (-30 - 10) \\\\\n12y &= -40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $x$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93dcc73c-ce2d-5fa3-b32b-9fc600fb269e" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7580cd-b447-5b07-8244-aa60ed8b432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218